### 6\. Core intelligence: Dust Priority Score

This is the feature I would highlight during your hackathon presentation.

Every reported location gets a priority score from 0 to 100.

P = 0.35S + 0.25F + 0.25D + 0.15U

| Variable | Meaning                  | Range |
| -------- | ------------------------ | ----- |
| S        | Observed dust severity   | 0–100 |
| F        | Frequency of reports     | 0–100 |
| D        | Duration unresolved      | 0–100 |
| U        | Urgency of the situation | 0–100 |

Example:

* Severity: 80

* Frequency: 70

* Duration: 90

* Urgency: 60

P=(0.35 \\times 80)+(0.25 \\times 70)+(0.25 \\times 90)+(0.15 \\times 60)

### 76.5 / 100

Illustrative priority score

You can initially use these categories:

| Score  | Priority |
| ------ | -------- |
| 0–39   | Low      |
| 40–69  | Medium   |
| 70–100 | High     |

These weights and thresholds are initial prototype assumptions, not validated pollution-risk measurements or official municipal standards. Repeated reports should also be checked for duplicates or abuse before they influence priority.

